# text
seg-c325655738e0 = What to send

# text
# c1: kshana.partner-submission/1
# c2: examples/partner-submission/submission.toml
seg-ca24fa511ffe = A submission is two files in one folder: the data and a small description of it, a TOML file (<c1/>; a worked, made-up example is <c2/>).

# text
# c1: ptp4l
# c2: phc2sys
# c3: ts2phc
seg-a3e30cf27adb = <b>Clock data.</b> One of the formats Kshana reads (see <a1>clock-log formats</a1>): TAPR TICC logs, TimeLab-style ASCII phase or frequency, RINEX clock, linuxptp <c1/>, <c2/> and <c3/> logs, chrony tracking and statistics logs. Phase is preferred over frequency; state the units and the sample interval. Give the longest uninterrupted record you have: stability at long averaging times needs days, and a record under a day is accepted but warned about.

# text
# c1: t_s, gx, gy, gz, ax, ay, az
seg-a14b0c961637 = <b>Inertial data.</b> A named-column CSV (<c1/>, rad/s and m/s^{ "2" }) or the EuRoC layout, from a stationary device, long enough for the fit (at least { "2000" } samples; hours is better).

# text
seg-a1f2cd79bcc0 = <b>The description</b> says, and the checker verifies where it can:

# text
seg-cfeee5da56d7 = the type of organisation (a type such as "a national timing laboratory", not a name; a name is optional and only for credit);

# text
# c1: other
seg-4bd30d68101c = the device (kind, class, and a model for class <c1/>);

# text
seg-e8222178a0ee = how the data was taken: what it was compared against, the start time (UTC), the environment, and whether it is raw or processed (and what was done to it);

# text
seg-7ac28bd566f2 = the SHA-256 of the data file, so the file scored is the file described;

# text
seg-0eba30c3a5b8 = the MODELLED rows it is offered against, by the exact requirement text above;

# text
# c1: public
# c2: hash-only
# c3: private
seg-dd694deec626 = the licence (an SPDX identifier or words) and how the data may be used in the repository: <c1/> (committed, with attribution), <c2/> (only its hash and the scores are published) or <c3/> (the score is shared with the submitter alone);

# text
seg-7cea4b7c1df8 = three statements: it is a measurement and not a simulation, the submitter may license it for this use, and the files hold no personal data.

# text
seg-caa6696f3f5f = <b>Not to send.</b> Anything personal or with contact details (the checker refuses an email address or telephone number in the description), anything under export control or restricted by agreement, and any simulated data presented as measured.

# text
seg-a2f0067b6da5 = How a submission is checked

# text
seg-f1d4c097691e = Format and metadata, by you or us, before anything runs.

# text
seg-c3cd90222eaa = It confirms the schema, the required fields, the hash, the licence and attribution rules, that the target rows exist and are MODELLED, that the data reads with the engine's own reader, and it reports what it read (samples, interval, span, gaps) and warns about records that are short. Exit status { "0" } means complete and readable; { "1" } lists what to fix. <b>It does not score the data, validate a row or promise it will be used.</b>

# text
# c1: xval/<name>/
seg-7dd95552a6fb = <b>A pre-registration, before any scored run.</b> For each row, a document in <c1/> fixes, and is committed before the data is run: the row, the data file's SHA-256, which part of the data is held out (nothing fitted may see it), the metric, and the bar. For clock cards the existing template is the held-out prediction used for the IGS and receiver records: fit on the first third of the record, predict the Allan deviation of the rest, and require the predicted over measured ratio within a stated factor at every fitted averaging time. A bar is never loosened after a run.

# text
seg-10dc9f8cf394 = <b>One scored run.</b> The result, every number and every miss included, is published with the command and the commit, and a test pins it. A miss is a finding, not a failure to hide: the row stays MODELLED and the page says so.

# text
seg-e5704c43a6b8 = <b>The ledger decision is the owner's</b>, in writing, in the commit that changes the row. The oracle text of a promoted row names the dataset, its licence and credit, what was compared and what was not.

# text
seg-6d467ffb4199 = Credit and rights

# text
# c1: hash-only
# c2: private
seg-e82f31e7d9cc = The data stays yours under the licence you state. <c1/> and <c2/> keep the file out of the repository.

# text
# c1: public
seg-156499ebc142 = A <c1/> submission is credited by the attribution line you give, in the row's oracle text and in the result file.

# text
seg-a5c01115d23a = Nothing is published under your name without your written consent; an organisation named as a source is cited as a source, never as an endorser. No claim of approval or endorsement is made by or about a partner.

# text
seg-6c9ed423e51d = You can withdraw a submission before its result is published. After that the result stays as recorded; if you withdraw the data licence, the file is removed from the repository and the hash and the scores remain.

# text
seg-2cc3c091ba0f = Kshana transmits nothing and drives no hardware. A submission is files in, a report out.

